===Etymology 1===
From English dialectal {{m|en|wanky}}, alteration of {{etyl|enm|en}} {{m|enm|wankel||unstable, shaky}}, from {{etyl|ang|en}} {{m|ang|wancol||unstable}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*wankulaz||swaying, shaky, unstable}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*wankōną||to sway, be unsteady}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{m|ine-pro|*wa(n)k-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*wek-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*wag-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*weg-||to swing, be unsteady, slant, be crooked}}. Cognate with {{cog|sco|wankle||wonky}}, {{cog|nl|wankel||shaky}}, {{cog|de|Wankelmut||fickleness, inconstancy, vacillation}}, {{cog|da|vanke||to wander}}. See also {{l|en|wankle}}.

